{{/* @dot donseba/go-webthings-docs/internal/website.DocsArticleData */}}
<article class="grid max-w-5xl gap-6">
    {{ template "hero.gohtml" . }}

<pre><code>im := importmap.NewDefaults().
    RootDir("/srv/my-site").
    CacheDir(".importmap").
    AssetsDir("assets")</code></pre>
<table><thead><tr><th>Purpose</th><th>Example path</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Package cache</td><td><code>/srv/my-site/.importmap/htmx/4.0.0/htmx.esm.min.js</code></td></tr><tr><td>Published asset</td><td><code>/srv/my-site/assets/htmx/htmx.esm.min.js</code></td></tr><tr><td>Browser URL</td><td><code>/assets/htmx/htmx.esm.min.js</code></td></tr></tbody></table>
<p><code>RootDir</code> controls reads and writes; it is not added to browser URLs. <code>AssetsDir</code> controls the published directory and, without <code>WithURLFunc</code>, the URL prefix. With a relative root, paths depend on the preparation process’s working directory.</p>
<p>These directories are for fetching at start-up. <a href="{{ basePath }}/vendoring" hx-get="{{ basePath }}/vendoring" hx-target="#content" hx-push-url="true">Vendored packages</a> live in the directory the <code>importmap</code> command writes, and <code>FromFS</code> reads them from an embedded file system.</p>
<h2>CacheOrFetch and Fetch</h2>
<p><code>CacheOrFetch(ctx)</code> needs both directories; <code>NewDefaults</code> sets them. It maps the packages that have a cache, rebuilds missing published files from the cache one by one, only the required ones, without contacting the provider, and fetches only the packages without a cache. A package without a version has no cache to find, so pin versions. <code>Fetch(ctx)</code> asks the providers for every package and downloads only the files not cached yet. A package whose includes are all <code>Raw</code> contacts no provider in either.</p>
<pre><code>if err := im.CacheOrFetch(ctx); err != nil { return err }</code></pre>
<p>Downloaded files are written through temporary files and published only after success. HTTP errors and context cancellation return errors instead of turning incomplete downloads into usable assets.</p>
<h2>Changing versions</h2>
<p>Cache directories include versions, while published paths do not. A published file that already exists is kept, so after changing a package pin, remove the package's directory below the assets directory before preparing again; the new version is then published at the same path. To give browsers the new file, fingerprint the published files after <code>CacheOrFetch</code> and pass the manifest to <code>WithAssets</code>: URLs then carry each file's version (<code>?v=…</code>) and its integrity is known.</p>
<pre><code>manifest, err := assets.Fingerprint(os.DirFS("/srv/my-site/assets"))
if err != nil { return err }
im.WithAssets(manifest)</code></pre>
<h2>Cleaning</h2>
<p><code>Clean()</code> removes the entire configured cache and assets directories and returns the import map for chaining. It is intended for directories owned by this preparation step. It can remove application assets stored in the same tree; do not use it as a routine request operation.</p>
</article>
